---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/findings.css";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (path: string) => `${base}${path.replace(/^\/+/, "")}`;

const leagueRows = [
  { label: "NFL", value: "15,518", width: 100 },
  { label: "NHL", value: "5,263", width: 33.9 },
  { label: "Premier League", value: "4,005", width: 25.8 },
  { label: "NBA", value: "2,866", width: 18.5 },
];

const coverageRows = [
  { label: "NBA", value: "100%", width: 100, warning: false },
  { label: "NHL", value: "99.9%", width: 99.9, warning: false },
  { label: "NFL", value: "98.9%", width: 98.9, warning: false },
  { label: "Premier League", value: "44.5%", width: 44.5, warning: true },
];

const roleRows = [
  { label: "NFL wide receiver", value: "2,017", width: 100 },
  { label: "NFL edge defender", value: "1,959", width: 97.1 },
  { label: "NHL defense", value: "1,638", width: 81.2 },
  { label: "NBA guard", value: "1,325", width: 65.7 },
  { label: "NHL goalie", value: "525", width: 26.0 },
  { label: "NBA center", value: "403", width: 20.0 },
  { label: "NFL long snapper", value: "214", width: 10.6 },
];

const opportunityRows = [
  { label: "NFL", note: "8,308 scored · 4,874 full-strength", scored: 53.5, eligible: 31.4 },
  { label: "NBA", note: "2,866 scored · 1,878 full-strength", scored: 100, eligible: 65.5 },
  { label: "NHL", note: "5,263 scored · 3,574 full-strength", scored: 100, eligible: 67.9 },
  { label: "Premier League", note: "2,760 scored · 1,690 full-strength", scored: 68.9, eligible: 42.2 },
];

const holdoutRows = [
  { label: "NFL edge", note: "113 players", model: 20.67, baseline: 25.91 },
  { label: "NBA guard", note: "132 players", model: 15.71, baseline: 25.26 },
  { label: "NHL center", note: "177 players", model: 14.74, baseline: 26.57 },
  { label: "Premier League midfielder", note: "83 players", model: 14.82, baseline: 25.98 },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project Findings | The Multi Sport Scouter"
  description="A report on exploratory analysis, modeling challenges, validation results, outcomes, and limitations."
>
  <section class="findings-hero">
    <div class="shell findings-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Project Report · frozen 2021–2025 study</p>
        <h1>Findings & Evaluation</h1>
        <p class="lead">The following page describes the project findings; noting interesting observations as well as challenges and outcomes I came across during the course of this project.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href="#data">Start with data collection</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="#outcomes">Jump to model outcomes</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="headline-finding" aria-label="Headline finding">
        <p class="eyebrow">TLDR</p>
        <strong>Summary of Findings</strong>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Cross-Sport Model are better at inference than prediction.</li>
            <li>Contextualizing player traits gives us a better chance of find cross-sport fits than body measurements alone.</li>
            <li>Training and using single sport models still produces the strongest predictive results.</li>
            <li>Our models in all four sports were able to reliably forecast future performances of players in percentiles.</li>
            <li>When applying those models on athletes from different sports, however, the predictive power is unreliable.</li>
            <li>Cross-sport scores do appear to be logical, empirically speaking: Creative players in Sport A have a higher likelihood of fitting creative, phsyically correct roles in Sport B.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="findings-jump shell" aria-label="Findings sections">
    <span>Report Sections</span>
    <a href="#data">Data</a>
    <a href="#eda">EDA</a>
    <a href="#challenges">Challenges</a>
    <a href="#models">Models</a>
    <a href="#evaluation">Evaluation</a>
    <a href="#outcomes">Outcomes</a>
  </nav>
  <section class="finding-section" id="data" aria-labelledby="data-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="findings-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">01 · Data Collection</p>
          <h2 id="data-heading">Profile of Initial Data</h2>
        </div>
        <!--<p>The panel covers five seasons, but each league contributes a different amount and quality of information. More rows improve the opportunity to learn patterns; they do not make every feature equally trustworthy.</p>-->
      </div>
      <figure class="report-chart role-chart" aria-labelledby="role-chart-title">
        <figcaption id="role-chart-title">Data Snapshot</figcaption>
        <p>For this project I aggregated data from a host of different sources (nflverse, FPLInsights, Moneypuck). The data snapshot used to train this model is shown below:</p>
        <div class="findings-snapshot" aria-label="Project snapshot">
          <dl>
            <div><dt>27,652</dt><dd>player-seasons</dd></div>
            <div><dt>10,311</dt><dd>sport-athlete identities</dd></div>
            <div><dt>4</dt><dd>top leagues</dd></div>
            <div><dt>26</dt><dd>registered broad roles</dd></div>
            <div><dt>2025</dt><dd>frozen test season</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="chart-pair">
        <figure class="report-chart" aria-labelledby="league-chart-title">
          <figcaption id="league-chart-title">Player-seasons by league</figcaption>
          <div class="simple-bars">
            {leagueRows.map((row) => (
              <div class="simple-bar-row">
                <span>{row.label}</span>
                <div class="bar-track" aria-hidden="true"><i style={`width: ${row.width}%`}></i></div>
                <strong>{row.value}</strong>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p>The NFL supplies more than half of the our snapshot data due to more readily available physical stat data. However, I was still able to find a decent amount of information for the other leagues.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="report-chart" aria-labelledby="coverage-chart-title">
          <figcaption id="coverage-chart-title">Rows with both listed height and weight</figcaption>
          <div class="simple-bars coverage-bars">
            {coverageRows.map((row) => (
              <div class={`simple-bar-row ${row.warning ? "warning-row" : ""}`}>
                <span>{row.label}</span>
                <div class="bar-track" aria-hidden="true"><i style={`width: ${row.width}%`}></i></div>
                <strong>{row.value}</strong>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p>While out data coverage per player per season is good, we still have some coverage gaps. The Premier League is the biggest issues where finding a comprehensive dataset on physical profiles for all seasons was hard.</p>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <figure class="report-chart role-chart" aria-labelledby="role-chart-title">
        <figcaption id="role-chart-title">Selected role cohorts show the sample-size gap</figcaption>
        <div class="role-bars">
          {roleRows.map((row) => (
            <div class="role-bar-row">
              <span>{row.label}</span>
              <div class="bar-track" aria-hidden="true"><i style={`width: ${row.width}%`}></i></div>
              <strong>{row.value}</strong>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p><strong>What it means:</strong> a role with about 2,000 seasons and one with about 200 cannot share the same confidence assumptions. The project evaluates each role separately and allows unsupported roles to abstain.</p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="finding-section tint" id="eda" aria-labelledby="eda-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="findings-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02 · Exploratory Data Analysis</p>
          <h2 id="eda-heading">What does our raw data tell us?</h2>
        </div>
        <p>I reviewed each sport separately before trying to compare them. For these examples I used the 2025 season and removed very small samples, so one short appearance would not create a misleading rate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="eda-insight-grid">
        <figure class="image-figure eda-insight">
          <div class="insight-heading"><span>NFL</span><h3>Winning possession looks different across one defense.</h3></div>
          <a href={href("images/eda/nfl_role_disruption_2025.png")} aria-label="Open the full-resolution NFL role disruption chart">
            <img src={href("images/eda/nfl_role_disruption_2025.png")} alt="Two horizontal bar charts comparing sacks and passes defended per 1,000 defensive opportunities for NFL edge defenders, interior defenders, linebackers, safeties and cornerbacks in 2025" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </a>
          <figcaption><strong>What stood out:</strong> across 583 defender seasons with at least 200 defensive opportunities, edge defenders recorded 8.43 sacks per 1,000 opportunities while cornerbacks recorded 12.77 passes defended. The same idea—disrupting possession—shows up through very different actions.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="image-figure eda-insight">
          <div class="insight-heading"><span>NBA</span><h3>Guards steal the ball; centers protect the rim.</h3></div>
          <a href={href("images/eda/nba_role_disruption_2025.png")} aria-label="Open the full-resolution NBA disruption chart">
            <img src={href("images/eda/nba_role_disruption_2025.png")} alt="Grouped bars comparing steals and blocks per 36 minutes for NBA guards, forwards and centers with at least 500 minutes in 2025" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </a>
          <figcaption><strong>What stood out:</strong> across 380 player seasons with at least 500 minutes, centers blocked 1.53 shots per 36 minutes—about 3.5 times the guard rate. Guards produced 1.36 steals per 36, about 32% more than centers.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="image-figure eda-insight">
          <div class="insight-heading"><span>NHL</span><h3>Defensemen stop shots while wings apply more contact.</h3></div>
          <a href={href("images/eda/nhl_role_physicality_2025.png")} aria-label="Open the full-resolution NHL role physicality chart">
            <img src={href("images/eda/nhl_role_physicality_2025.png")} alt="Grouped bars comparing blocked shots and hits per 60 minutes for NHL centers, defensemen, left wings and right wings with at least 600 minutes in 2025" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </a>
          <figcaption><strong>What stood out:</strong> across 578 skater seasons with at least 600 minutes, defensemen blocked 4.21 shots per 60—more than twice either wing. Left wings led contact at 5.01 hits per 60, about 68% above defensemen.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="image-figure eda-insight">
          <div class="insight-heading"><span>Premier League</span><h3>Listed build separates some roles even before performance is added.</h3></div>
          <a href={href("images/eda/epl_build_by_role_2025.png")} aria-label="Open the full-resolution Premier League height and weight chart">
            <img src={href("images/eda/epl_build_by_role_2025.png")} alt="Scatter plot of listed height and weight for Premier League players in 2025, grouped by goalkeeper, defender, midfielder and forward, with role medians marked" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </a>
          <figcaption><strong>What stood out:</strong> the median goalkeeper was 190.5 cm and 83.0 kg, roughly 10.2 cm taller and 10.0 kg heavier than the median midfielder. This chart covers 500 of 841 player rows with both fields, so it shows the observed sample—not the full league.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="eda-method-note"><strong>How this shaped the project:</strong> I could not treat every possession-winning action as the same thing. I first measured players against their own sport, role and season, then translated only broader themes such as disruption, creation and recovery across sports.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="finding-section" id="models" aria-labelledby="models-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="findings-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03 · Model Building</p>
          <h2 id="models-heading">I built the model in stages so every result has a clear meaning.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>I used simple linear models because I wanted to show what information was being used and compare each version with an easy benchmark. A more complicated model would not fix missing data or a weak definition of success.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="model-flow">
        <li><span>1</span><strong>Score the completed season</strong><p>I converted performance into a percentile within the same sport, role and season.</p></li>
        <li><span>2</span><strong>Account for playing time</strong><p>Players with fewer snaps or minutes stayed in the data, but they had less influence.</p></li>
        <li><span>3</span><strong>Use earlier information</strong><p>The next-season model used build and playing information that was already available.</p></li>
        <li><span>4</span><strong>Keep 2025 hidden</strong><p>I made the model choices first, then used 2025 as the final unseen test.</p></li>
        <li><span>5</span><strong>Compare with something simple</strong><p>The model had to improve on a middle-of-the-pack, recent-history or build-only estimate.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="model-questions">
        <article><span>Completed season</span><h3>How did the player perform?</h3><p>The player is ranked against peers in the same sport, role and season.</p></article>
        <article><span>Next season</span><h3>What does the model expect next?</h3><p>Earlier seasons are used to estimate the player's next same-role percentile.</p></article>
        <article><span>Body comparison</span><h3>Does the player resemble the role?</h3><p>Listed height, weight, body mass and age are compared with the target role.</p></article>
        <article><span>Other sports</span><h3>What role does the profile resemble?</h3><p>Build and a small set of shared playing themes are used as a scouting comparison.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="subsection-heading" id="challenges">
        <p class="eyebrow">Model challenges</p>
        <h3>The main challenge was keeping useful low-minute players without trusting them too much.</h3>
        <p>I kept seasons with positive playing time, but reduced their influence. I only used the stronger sample below when deciding how well a model performed.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-legend" aria-label="Opportunity chart legend">
        <span><i class="legend-swatch scored" aria-hidden="true"></i>Season score available</span>
        <span><i class="legend-swatch eligible" aria-hidden="true"></i>Strong enough for final testing</span>
      </div>
      <figure class="report-chart opportunity-chart" aria-labelledby="opportunity-title">
        <figcaption id="opportunity-title">How much of each sport's data was strong enough for each use</figcaption>
        {opportunityRows.map((row) => (
          <div class="opportunity-row">
            <div class="opportunity-label"><strong>{row.label}</strong><span>{row.note}</span></div>
            <div class="paired-bars" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="bar-track"><i class="scored-fill" style={`width: ${row.scored}%`}></i></div>
              <div class="bar-track"><i class="eligible-fill" style={`width: ${row.eligible}%`}></i></div>
            </div>
            <div class="paired-values"><span>{row.scored}%</span><span>{row.eligible}%</span></div>
          </div>
        ))}
        <p><strong>What it means:</strong> I could describe 19,197 player seasons, but I used only 12,016 stronger samples to judge the models. Another 7,181 low-sample rows helped with training at a reduced weight without deciding whether a model passed.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="challenge-grid">
        <article><strong>Every sport measures opportunity differently</strong><p>Snaps, court minutes, ice time and football minutes cannot be treated as the same unit.</p></article>
        <article><strong>Listed build is usually a snapshot</strong><p>Repeating one height or weight beside several seasons does not create a real body history.</p></article>
        <article><strong>More information did not always help</strong><p>I kept an added group of features only when it improved the unseen-season result.</p></article>
        <article><strong>True sport switches are missing</strong><p>There are not enough documented elite transfers among these four sports to train on real switch outcomes.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="evaluation-section" id="evaluation" aria-labelledby="evaluation-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="findings-heading light-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">04 · Validation, Evaluation & Ensemble Build</p>
          <h2 id="evaluation-heading">I tested the same-sport models first, then built the cross-sport comparison.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>The model trained on seasons before 2025. I kept 2025 out of the model-building decisions and used it at the end to see how far the forecasts missed. Lower error is better.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="evidence-summary" aria-label="Model evidence summary">
        <article><strong>4 / 23</strong><span>body-only role models clearly beat their simple comparison</span></article>
        <article><strong>11 / 23</strong><span>next-season models clearly beat a neutral 50th-percentile guess</span></article>
        <article><strong>11 / 23</strong><span>build plus playing style improved on build alone</span></article>
        <article class="warning-evidence"><strong>0 / 23</strong><span>passed every rule needed for a deployment-grade forecast</span></article>
      </div>

      <div class="dark-legend" aria-label="Holdout chart legend">
        <span><i class="legend-swatch model" aria-hidden="true"></i>Our next-season model</span>
        <span><i class="legend-swatch baseline" aria-hidden="true"></i>Simple 50th-percentile guess</span>
      </div>
      <figure class="holdout-chart" aria-labelledby="holdout-title">
        <figcaption id="holdout-title">Average miss on the unseen 2025 season · percentile points · lower is better</figcaption>
        {holdoutRows.map((row) => (
          <div class="holdout-row">
            <div><strong>{row.label}</strong><span>{row.note}</span></div>
            <div class="holdout-bars" aria-hidden="true">
              <div><i class="model-fill" style={`width: ${(row.model / 30) * 100}%`}></i></div>
              <div><i class="baseline-fill" style={`width: ${(row.baseline / 30) * 100}%`}></i></div>
            </div>
            <div class="holdout-values"><span>{row.model}</span><span>{row.baseline}</span></div>
          </div>
        ))}
        <p><strong>What this tells me:</strong> these examples show that the model learned useful same-sport patterns. They do not prove that a player could move to another sport, and several role models still did not beat the player's own recent history.</p>
      </figure>

      <figure class="image-figure profile-figure evaluation-figure">
        <a href={href("images/eda/profile_increment_vs_build.png")} aria-label="Open the full-resolution playing-profile improvement chart">
          <img src={href("images/eda/profile_increment_vs_build.png")} alt="Horizontal bars showing how much prior playing profile improved or worsened next-season error compared with using build alone for each role" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </a>
        <figcaption><strong>Playing style improved 11 of 23 role models over build alone.</strong> It helped several NBA, NHL and Premier League roles, while some NFL roles stayed flat or became worse. I kept that decision separate for every role instead of assuming that more data must help.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="subsection-heading light-subsection">
        <p class="eyebrow">The ensemble model</p>
        <h3>For the final layer, I compared a player's build and playing profile with roles in the other three sports.</h3>
        <p>I grouped sport-specific statistics into a small number of shared ideas, such as scoring, creation, disruption and recovery. The source and target role needed at least two shared ideas before I produced a comparison.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="model-flow ensemble-flow">
        <li><span>1</span><strong>Start with the player's own profile</strong><p>I use listed build and completed-season playing information.</p></li>
        <li><span>2</span><strong>Find shared ideas</strong><p>I match only themes that have a reasonable meaning in both the source and target roles.</p></li>
        <li><span>3</span><strong>Use another sport's role model</strong><p>The player's native sport is left out of this step so it cannot distort the comparison.</p></li>
        <li><span>4</span><strong>Show a resemblance score</strong><p>The output describes similarity to the target role, not the chance of making a roster.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <figure class="bridge-result dark-bridge" aria-labelledby="bridge-title">
        <figcaption id="bridge-title">How far the cross-sport evaluation can honestly go</figcaption>
        <div class="bridge-flow">
          <div><strong>286</strong><span>source-role to target-role comparisons built</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div><strong>132</strong><span>added useful information beyond build alone in the target sport</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div class="bridge-stop"><strong>0</strong><span>real sport-switch outcomes available for validation</span></div>
        </div>
        <p><strong>The important boundary:</strong> the ensemble can find an interesting physical and playing-style comparison. It cannot estimate the probability that an athlete learns the new sport, makes a team or reaches that percentile.</p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="finding-section" id="outcomes" aria-labelledby="outcomes-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="findings-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">05 · Outcomes & Conclusion</p>
          <h2 id="outcomes-heading">The project works best as a discovery tool for scouts and analysts.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>The strongest result was not one universal rating. It was a workflow that first checks whether the model understands a player in their own sport, then uses other-sport comparisons to suggest roles worth investigating.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="outcome-grid">
        <article class="outcome-card positive"><span>What worked</span><h3>Same-sport context added real value</h3><p>Role, season, playing time and recent performance produced sensible current ratings and useful 2025 forecasts for several roles.</p></article>
        <article class="outcome-card mixed"><span>What was limited</span><h3>Body type helped with fit more than quality</h3><p>Height and weight often identified the right type of role, but similar builds could still produce very different levels of performance.</p></article>
        <article class="outcome-card negative"><span>What remains unknown</span><h3>Cross-sport success is not yet predictable</h3><p>The comparisons are logical enough to create a scouting lead, but no real switch dataset exists to prove that the score predicts success.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="sanity-section">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">A simple confidence check</p>
          <h3>Known strengths appeared where I expected them.</h3>
          <p>Stephen Curry scored highly for scoring and LeBron James scored highly for creation. These checks do not validate a sport switch, but they help catch mistakes in player identity, role assignment and feature construction.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sanity-cards">
          <article><span>Stephen Curry</span><strong>92.9</strong><p>scoring and finishing percentile</p></article>
          <article><span>LeBron James</span><strong>97.4</strong><p>creation and distribution percentile</p></article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="limitations-grid">
        <article class="limitations-panel">
          <p class="eyebrow">Current limitations</p>
          <h3>What the model still cannot see</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Most height and weight values are current listings, not measurements taken every season.</li>
            <li>Public box scores do not directly measure reaction speed, decision-making, technique or learning ability.</li>
            <li>Premier League physical coverage is incomplete and several public sources have reuse restrictions.</li>
            <li>The roles are intentionally broad because the four sports do not share equally detailed tactical data.</li>
            <li>There is no large, reliable set of athletes who actually attempted these sport switches.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <aside class="next-steps">
          <p class="eyebrow">Next improvements</p>
          <h3>What I would add in version two</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Dated combine, agility and reaction testing across all four sports.</li>
            <li>Richer Premier League event, lineup and tracking data.</li>
            <li>Repeatable coach or scout ratings for awareness, technique and learning.</li>
            <li>More detailed validation for new players and rare roles.</li>
            <li>A real dataset of athletes who trained or competed in another sport.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <div class="final-conclusion">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The conclusion</p>
          <h2>The model should start a scouting conversation, not finish one.</h2>
          <p>I can use it to identify a player whose body type and playing profile resemble a role in another sport. The next step should still be film review, physical testing, technical evaluation and feedback from coaches.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href={href("stories/")}>Present the model stories</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={href("explorer/")}>Open the player explorer</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
